5
00:00:24,920 --> 00:00:29,980
Sometimes it is. Usually it isn't. But the signing part is always affordable.

6
00:00:30,900 --> 00:00:39,220
What Treaties Actually Cost. N.G.O. campaign budgets are rarely disclosed publicly, but treaty campaigns run on pocket change

7
00:00:39,220 --> 00:00:44,960
compared to the thing they create. The Ottawa Treaty banning landmines took fourteen months.

8
00:00:45,840 --> 00:00:51,800
One staff member, Jody Williams, grew a coalition to over a thousand organizations across sixty countries.

9
00:00:52,860 --> 00:00:57,200
One hundred and twenty-two states signed. She won the Nobel Peace Prize.

10
00:00:58,140 --> 00:01:05,560
One woman with a phone convinced one hundred and twenty-two countries to stop burying explosives in the ground where children

11
00:01:05,560 --> 00:01:12,320
walk. Your species is capable of remarkable things when one person gets sufficiently annoyed.

12
00:01:13,380 --> 00:01:20,580
The Cluster Munitions Convention took about two years. Seven governments and a coalition of N.G.O.s got ninety-four states

13
00:01:20,580 --> 00:01:26,360
to sign. The Arms Trade Treaty took a decade but started with three supporting governments.

14
00:01:27,040 --> 00:01:33,300
The Control Arms coalition published fifty plus reports until one hundred and thirty countries signed.

15
00:01:34,240 --> 00:01:42,720
The Nuclear Ban Treaty also took a decade. The International Campaign to Abolish Nuclear Weapons, or I.C.A.N., ran the entire

16
00:01:42,720 --> 00:01:49,000
