155
00:17:36,120 --> 00:17:41,740
Why This Differs from Every Other Treaty. Zero point seven percent O.D.A.

156
00:17:41,740 --> 00:17:49,300
failed because nobody made money when countries complied. The N.G.O.s pushing for compliance have small budgets, compete

157
00:17:49,300 --> 00:17:53,990
with each other, and run on guilt. Guilt has terrible fuel efficiency.

158
00:17:54,500 --> 00:17:58,440
Guilt gets you to the gym in January. Greed gets you there in July.

159
00:17:59,280 --> 00:18:05,620
Guilt volunteers at the soup kitchen once. Greed builds the soup kitchen and names it after itself.

160
00:18:06,200 --> 00:18:10,360
Guilt is what your species feels. Greed is what your species does.

161
00:18:11,200 --> 00:18:18,850
This treaty runs on what your species does. The one percent health treaty creates its own expansion lobby.

162
00:18:19,280 --> 00:18:24,600
Bond holders profit when the treaty expands to two percent, then five percent, then ten percent.

163
00:18:25,320 --> 00:18:32,080
These aren't activists you can ignore at a dinner party. They're investors with quarterly earnings calls about curing diseases.

164
00:18:32,900 --> 00:18:39,620
They have Bloomberg terminals. They have lobbyists. They have the same tools that defense contractors currently use to expand

165
00:18:39,620 --> 00:18:43,340
military budgets, except pointed in the opposite direction.

166
00:18:43,960 --> 00:18:51,740
The Ratchet Effect. Once one percent passes, two point seven two billion dollars a year flows to I.A.B.

167
00:18:51,780 --> 00:18:58,860
holders pushing for two percent. When two percent passes, five point four four billion dollars a year pushes for five percent.

168
00:18:59,360 --> 00:19:04,820
When five percent passes, thirteen point six billion dollars a year pushes for ten percent.

169
00:19:05,560 --> 00:19:12,960
The political pressure for expansion accelerates with each success, because each expansion funds a larger lobbying force

170
00:19:12,960 --> 00:19:19,320
for the next expansion, because the people who got rich at one percent want to get richer at two percent, and the people

171
00:19:19,320 --> 00:19:27,160
who got rich at two percent want to get richer at five percent, and so on until the entire engine runs on compound greed,

172
00:19:27,680 --> 00:19:35,280
which is like compound interest but for not dying. Most treaties are gym memberships purchased in January and abandoned

173
00:19:35,280 --> 00:19:42,340
by February. I.A.B.s are the opposite: the constituency for expansion grows with each success.

174
00:19:43,140 --> 00:19:49,880
Greed doesn't take holidays. Greed doesn't forget. Greed doesn't lose interest when a new television show comes on.

175
00:19:50,700 --> 00:19:55,180
Greed is the only human motivation that has never once failed to show up.

176
00:19:55,860 --> 00:20:00,130
This treaty runs on it. On Wishonia, we find this arrangement embarrassing.

177
00:20:00,880 --> 00:20:06,600
On Earth, it's the only arrangement that works. Long-Term Feasibility.

178
00:20:06,700 --> 00:20:11,580
With I.A.B.s, the ten percent allocation creates a perpetual political engine.

179
00:20:12,040 --> 00:20:17,700
A machine that runs on greed and outputs health. Embarrassing, but effective.

180
00:20:17,760 --> 00:20:21,800
The most effective things your species has ever built have all been embarrassing.

181
00:20:22,320 --> 00:20:27,160
The internet was built for sharing research papers and is used for arguing with strangers.

182
00:20:27,840 --> 00:20:32,660
Democracy was built for collective wisdom and is used for electing celebrities.

183
00:20:33,420 --> 00:20:37,810
This machine is built for curing disease and is powered by selfishness.

184
00:20:38,220 --> 00:20:46,580
The pattern holds. Without I.A.B.s, this joins Kyoto in the Museum of Things Humanity Promised to Do and Then Didn't.

185
00:20:47,260 --> 00:20:52,820
The museum is very large. It has a gift shop. The gift shop sells promises.
